1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,839
Welcome to this new analysis; today we are going to explore a recently published paper,

2
00:00:04,839 --> 00:00:10,120
none other than in the prestigious Review of Economic Studies, a work that I warn you

3
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:15,200
will make you reflect deeply. We will indeed talk about one of those consequences of economic crises

4
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:20,120
that is almost never discussed, but which is absolutely crucial: the hidden link

5
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:24,839
between job loss and the increase in domestic violence.

6
00:00:24,839 --> 00:00:29,560
So, let's start with the basics: what truly happens within the home when suddenly

7
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:34,960
someone loses their job? Of course, we usually think immediately of the financial aspect, the bank account

8
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:39,520
that suffers, but the data we are about to unravel reveals a much darker side,

9
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:45,359
the human and domestic costs of these economic shocks. Thanks to a gigantic and

10
00:00:45,359 --> 00:00:50,240
super rigorous analysis carried out on administrative records in Brazil, a complex reality emerges

11
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:52,200
that truly affects us deeply.

12
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:58,079
And let's start immediately with a number that will literally make you jump out of your seat. When it's the man who loses

13
00:00:58,079 --> 01:02:320
his job due to mass layoffs, the risk of him committing acts of domestic violence

14
01:02:320 --> 01:09:359
skyrockets by 32%. You heard that right: a 32% jump.

15
01:09:359 --> 01:14:479
It's clear proof of how the tension and anxiety generated by unemployment transform

16
01:14:479 --> 01:19:359
into real physical conflicts. But wait, because here comes the data that

17
01:19:359 --> 01:25:039
disrupts classical economic theories: if it's the woman who loses her job, well, the probability

18
01:25:040 --> 01:29:920
that she becomes a victim of domestic violence shoots up by 56%.

19
01:29:920 --> 01:34:400
Incredible, right? Many would think that by changing who brings money home,

20
01:34:400 --> 01:39:600
the power dynamics would also change in a mirrored way. But no, the data speaks for itself.

21
01:39:600 --> 01:44:400
In both cases, a tragic and dramatic spike in abuse against women is triggered.

22
01:44:400 --> 01:48:240
And mind you, we are not talking about a fleeting moment of crisis.

23
01:48:240 --> 01:52:680
The research data shows us a surge in legal proceedings immediately

24
01:52:680 --> 01:57:320
after the layoff, and up to this point, we might even expect it. But the shocking thing is

25
01:57:320 --> 02:02:680
that the risk does not decrease; it remains extremely high, stubbornly elevated for a full 4 years after

26
02:02:680 --> 02:07:480
that initial shock. This gives us the exact measure of how deep and literally

27
02:07:480 --> 02:10:560
lasting the wound left by job loss is.

28
02:10:560 --> 02:15:319
Now, we might ask ourselves, couldn't it be that only the propensity to report changes

29
02:15:319 --> 02:20:159
depending on who is employed? Absolutely not. The researchers did not stop at the courts;

30
02:20:159 --> 02:24:599
they went to comb through the healthcare system data. In Brazil, when doctors find

31
02:24:599 --> 02:29:199
abuse, they are legally obliged to report it, regardless of what the victim wants to do,

32
02:29:199 --> 02:33:680
and guess what? The medical reports confirm to the millimeter the surge in violence

33
02:33:680 --> 02:38:800
seen earlier. No statistical error here; we are facing a real and tangible drama.

34
02:38:800 --> 02:45:039
All this leads us directly to the million-dollar question: Why? Why do these peaks occur

35
02:45:039 --> 02:50:120
regardless of who loses their job in the couple? What's behind it? What are

36
02:50:120 --> 02:56:120
the psychological, practical, or economic levers that lead to the exact same result for both

37
02:56:120 --> 03:00:920
male and female layoffs? To understand this, economists have

38
03:00:920 --> 03:06:080
decomposed the problem by identifying two very powerful forces, almost in opposition to

39
03:06:080 --> 03:11:159
each other. On one hand, there's the so-called income shock, which is the devastating psychological stress

40
03:11:159 --> 03:15:439
of suddenly finding oneself without money, pushing the family's mental resilience

41
03:15:439 --> 03:20:240
to the brink. On the other hand, and this is the real novelty, there is the exposure shock.

42
03:20:240 --> 03:25:120
No job means spending many more hours confined at home,

43
03:25:120 --> 03:30:159
and this extra time creates friction, a constant and palpable emotional tension.

44
03:30:159 --> 03:34:680
Let's first look at the income shock. Immediately after collective layoffs, formal income

45
03:34:680 --> 03:40:759
drops by approximately 70% compared to the initial level. A 70% cut, practically,

46
03:40:759 --> 03:45:120
overnight, all financial safety nets vanish. And as you can imagine,

47
03:45:120 --> 03:49:879
this sudden loss becomes a huge, overwhelming stress factor for the entire

48
03:49:879 --> 03:53:840
family unit. But let's move on to the second force, the mechanism

49
03:53:840 --> 03:59:360
of exposure, which literally works like an increase in pressure. Sudden

50
03:59:360 --> 04:04:400
unemployment forces partners to spend a lot of time together, interacting

51
04:04:400 --> 04:09:879
in an environment already full of tension due to lack of money. Imagine finding yourselves

52
04:09:879 --> 04:14:560
sharing the same space. All day, every day, consumed by anxiety about the

53
04:14:560 --> 04:20:319
future. The chances of getting into arguments unfortunately multiply exponentially.

54
04:20:319 --> 04:25:480
At this point, a question naturally arises: Shouldn't public policies

55
04:25:480 --> 04:30:959
help us? I mean, don't classic social safety nets like unemployment benefits serve

56
04:30:959 --> 04:35:120
precisely to prevent families from falling not only into poverty but also into this

57
04:35:120 --> 04:40:240
dangerous spiral of domestic violence? To clarify, let's focus for a moment

58
04:40:240 --> 04:45:280
on the timeline of the benefits analyzed in this study. In the first month, job loss occurs.

59
04:45:280 --> 04:50:280
From the first to the fifth month, the state regularly pays the benefit, which covers

60
04:50:280 --> 04:55:040
a good portion of the lost salary. Then, from the sixth month onwards, these benefits

61
04:55:040 --> 04:59:920
completely run out. Keeping this precise timeline in mind is absolutely

62
04:59:920 --> 05:04:600
crucial to understanding how income and exposure play their part over the

63
05:04:600 --> 05:11:000
months. And it is precisely here that an astonishing paradox emerges. On the one hand, unemployment benefits

64
05:11:000 --> 05:16:120
do exactly what they were created for: they alleviate the income shock by bringing

65
05:16:120 --> 05:21:480
a bit of oxygen to finances and reducing stress. Fantastic, right? But there's a huge

66
05:21:480 --> 05:27:080
but. By guaranteeing an income, the benefit indirectly removes the urgency to immediately seek

67
05:27:080 --> 05:32:680
new employment. Thus, partners remain at home much longer. In practice, it prolongs

68
05:32:680 --> 05:38:319
the exposure shock indefinitely. Let's line up the stages of this dynamic. Step

69
05:38:319 --> 05:43:600
1. As long as unemployment benefits arrive, the two forces somehow cancel each other out.

70
05:43:600 --> 05:48:840
The bank account holds up, stress decreases, and this balances the risk due to the fact

71
05:48:840 --> 05:55:079
of spending much more time at home. Consequently, the rate of violence remains stable. Step 2.

72
05:55:079 --> 06:01:439
The benefit expires. Suddenly, zero income, but exposure is still at an all-time high

73
06:01:439 --> 06:06:399
because unemployment has now become entrenched. The result of this explosive mix

74
06:06:399 --> 06:11:519
is a sharp and frightening peak of violence. The gist of the entire discovery can be summarized

75
06:11:519 --> 06:17:639
with the researchers' own words. I quote: Unemployment benefits do not reduce

76
06:17:639 --> 06:22:360
domestic violence and lead to a greater risk once they expire. This is a truly

77
06:22:360 --> 06:27:519
strong statement. It confronts us with the dark and entirely unintentional consequences of a social policy

78
06:27:519 --> 06:32:000
designed for good, as a safety net, but one that shows profound limitations

79
06:32:000 --> 06:36:759
if we don't look beyond simple accounting figures. And to conclude, I leave you

80
06:36:759 --> 06:41:439
with a truly necessary reflection. If benefits alone end up prolonging

81
06:41:439 --> 06:46:079
isolation, without resolving the real risk of abuse, how do we truly protect

82
06:46:079 --> 06:50:799
these families during layoffs? The study clearly suggests that providing funds

83
06:50:799 --> 06:55:919
is essential, certainly, but it's not enough. Perhaps we should think about more robust policies

84
06:55:920 --> 07:00:640
that incentivize a quick return to work, or active training programs, that reduce

85
07:00:640 --> 07:05:319
that time of domestic inactivity. In short, we must rethink how we support

86
07:05:319 --> 07:10:439
those who lose their jobs, to ensure real and comprehensive security. Had you

87
07:10:439 --> 07:11:120
ever thought about this?